// ============================================================================
// Resolution · CONTEXTUAL PARAMETER TYPING  (the callback's parameter)
//
// `xs.filter((a) => a.tag())` — `a` carries no written type. Its type is the type of
// the parameter of the SIGNATURE the arrow is being passed to, with the callee's type
// variables substituted from the RECEIVER. Without it the arrow's body is untyped and
// every call inside it dies: 12 sites in the typeflow fixture, and `map` / `filter` /
// `reduce` / `forEach` callbacks are the most common higher-order shape there is.
//
// generics.dl already substitutes on the RETURN side — `find(): T | undefined` bound
// from the receiver's declared reference — and its header excludes the parameter side
// by name. This is that other side, built from the same four relations it uses:
// expr_decl_ref, type_arg_binding, owner_declares_param, ref_type_target.
//
// ── WHY IT IS MODELLED ON THE JAVA ENGINE ───────────────────────────────────
// java/expression-resolution/lambda.dl solves the identical problem and has already
// paid for the mistakes. Its shape is (1) find the target signature from the callee's
// parameter at the argument's position, (2) take that signature's k-th parameter, and
// (3) where that parameter is a type VARIABLE rather than a concrete type, bind it —
// its clause (3c) notes the variable is usually bound "not on the param type but by the
// RECEIVER's generics (Pipe<LSink> → T=LSink)", which is exactly `Array<Alpha>.filter`.
// Both clauses below are kept for the same reason Java keeps both: a concrete signature
// parameter needs no binding, and a generic one cannot resolve without it.
//
// Java's clause (i-b) is the warning worth repeating. The JDK writes
// `forEach(Consumer<? super T>)`, so the variable sits a level deeper than the obvious
// one and their depth-1 rule matched NOTHING — `fi_tvar_arg` was 0 rows for every JDK
// higher-order method until the wildcard was made transparent. TypeScript's equivalent
// is `readonly T[]` and `ReadonlyArray<T>` in a parameter position; if this file
// measures as no-change, that nesting is the first thing to look at.
//
// ── STRATIFICATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Positive throughout — no negation. expr_type ⇄ expr_call_candidate ⇄
// lambda_param_type ⇄ expr_type is mutual recursion in one stratum, the same shape
// generics.dl already sits in.
// ============================================================================

// ── the argument that is a function value, and the declaration it introduces ─
fn_value_kind("ARROW_FUNCTION").
fn_value_kind("FUNCTION_EXPRESSION").

callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm) :- expr_child("client", ce, "ARGUMENT", argpos, lam),
    expr_kind("client", k, _, lam),
    fn_value_kind(k),
    expr_anon_decl("client", lm, lam).

// ── the signature the callback is passed to, read off the TYPE REFERENCE ───
// An inline `(value: T, index: number, array: T[]) => unknown` is NOT a declaration:
// it has no resolved type, no shape, and no call-signature method — measured, there are
// ZERO call-signature methods in the whole staged standard library. Routing through
// call_signature_in_scope therefore produced nothing at all, twice, before this was
// checked. What the parser does emit is the function type as a FUNCTION_TYPE reference
// whose PARAMETER TYPES are its positional children:
//
//   FUNCTION_TYPE  ─ 0 TYPE_VARIABLE T   ─ 1 PRIMITIVE number
//                  ─ 2 ARRAY             ─ 3 PRIMITIVE unknown   ← the RETURN type
//
// So the k-th parameter is the k-th child. The last child is the return and is not a
// parameter, which needs no guard: TypeScript forbids a callback with more parameters
// than its target signature, so a lambda parameter's position can never reach it.
callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref) :- param_decl(_, _, argpos, _, callee, p),
    param_type_ref(_, fref, p),
    type_ref(_, "FUNCTION_TYPE", _, _, _, _, _, fref).

fn_param_tvar(fref, k, pname) :- type_ref_parent(_, fref, k, child),
    type_ref(_, "TYPE_VARIABLE", _, pname, _, _, _, child),
    pname != "".
fn_param_type(fref, k, prov, t) :- type_ref_parent(_, fref, k, child),
    ref_type_target(child, prov, t).

// ── bind the callback's parameters ──────────────────────────────────────────
// (a) CONCRETE — the signature names a real type, so no substitution is needed.
lambda_param_type(lp, prov, t) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    fn_param_type(fref, k, prov, t),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).

// (a2) …AND WHERE THAT PARAMETER IS ITSELF A FUNCTION TYPE, which `ref_type_target`
// cannot answer. `new Promise((resolve, reject) => { ... resolve(v) ... })` is the shape:
// the executor's own parameters are callbacks, so the k-th child of the executor's
// FUNCTION_TYPE is another FUNCTION_TYPE and not a named type. Clause (a) resolves the
// child through `ref_type_target`, which names declarations, so it found nothing and
// `resolve` carried no type — every call through it resolved to nothing.
//
// A SHAPE rather than a type, because that is what makes it callable: `param_shape_target`
// is what `expr_shape` reads for a parameter reference, and `call_signature_in_scope` then
// finds the FUNCTION_TYPE's own signature. The target is exactly what the compiler names —
// the `resolve` and `reject` parameter signatures inside PromiseConstructor's executor.
//
// Measured before writing it: 112 missed sites across three of the six corpus projects
// resolve, per the compiler, into `lib.es2015.promise.d.ts` — 88 of them a called
// executor parameter (`resolve`/`reject`, or whatever the callback names them: `ok` and
// `fail` account for most of one project's) and the rest `Promise.resolve`/`reject`
// themselves. It generalises by construction: any callback whose own parameter is a
// callback takes this path, which `new Promise` merely happens to be the commonest
// instance of.
lambda_param_shape(lp, sh) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    type_ref_parent(_, fref, k, child),
    ref_shape_target(child, sh),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).
param_shape_target(lp, sh) :- lambda_param_shape(lp, sh).

// (b) GENERIC — the signature's k-th parameter is a type VARIABLE, bound by the
// RECEIVER's own type arguments. This is java/lambda.dl clause (3c): the variable is
// bound "not on the param type but by the RECEIVER's generics".
lambda_param_type(lp, prov, t) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    ts_call_site(_, _, _, recv, _, ce, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    recv != "",
    expr_decl_ref(recv, rref),
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    fn_param_tvar(fref, k, pname),
    owner_declares_param(callee, pname),
    type_arg_binding(rref, pname, argRef),
    ref_type_target(argRef, prov, t),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).

// (b2/c2) …AND WHERE THE BOUND ARGUMENT IS ITSELF A FUNCTION TYPE. `fns: readonly
// ((data: T) => boolean)[]` then `fns.every((fn) => fn(1))`: the signature's parameter is
// the type variable, the variable binds to the array's ELEMENT, and that element is a
// function type. Clauses (b) and (c) bind it as a TYPE through ref_type_target / 
// ref_element_target, which name declarations, so a function-typed element bound nothing
// and calling `fn` resolved to nothing — while the compiler answers the signature written
// in the annotation.
//
// A SHAPE for the same reason (a2) needs one: param_shape_target is what expr_shape reads
// for a parameter reference, and call_signature_in_scope then finds the FUNCTION_TYPE's
// own signature. owner_declares_param is kept from the clauses this mirrors, so `every`'s
// callback parameter binds Array's own variable and `reduce`'s U, declared on the method,
// still does not.
lambda_param_shape(lp, sh) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    ts_call_site(_, _, _, recv, _, ce, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    recv != "",
    expr_decl_ref(recv, rref),
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    fn_param_tvar(fref, k, pname),
    owner_declares_param(callee, pname),
    ref_element_shape(rref, sh),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).

// (d) …AND WHERE THE RECEIVER IS AN EXPRESSION RATHER THAN A DECLARED REFERENCE.
// `words.filter(p).map((word) => word.slice(1))` — clauses (b) and (c) bind the callee's
// type variable from `expr_decl_ref(recv, rref)`, the receiver's DECLARATION reference,
// so they reach a receiver written as a name and not one written as a chained call. The
// element type of a call result is already derivable — `expr_element_type` takes it from
// the callee's declared return — and nothing was asking it this question.
//
// Measured: of the 145 missed sites whose receiver is an unannotated parameter, 87 (60%)
// have their arrow in ARGUMENT position already, so clause (a)-(c) should have fired and
// could not. This is why.
//
// Reads the RECEIVER EXPRESSION's element type rather than its reference, so it composes
// with every way an element type is derived — an annotation, a call's return, a getter's
// return, the iteration protocol — instead of only with a declared container reference.
// owner_declares_param is kept from the clauses this mirrors, so `map`'s callback
// parameter binds the container's own variable and `reduce`'s U, declared on the method,
// still does not.
lambda_param_type(lp, prov, t) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    ts_call_site(_, _, _, recv, _, ce, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    recv != "",
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    fn_param_tvar(fref, k, pname),
    owner_declares_param(callee, pname),
    expr_element_type(recv, prov, t),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).
// The shape twin, for an element that is itself a function type — the (a2)/(b2) reason.
lambda_param_shape(lp, sh) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    ts_call_site(_, _, _, recv, _, ce, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    recv != "",
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    fn_param_tvar(fref, k, pname),
    owner_declares_param(callee, pname),
    expr_element_shape(recv, sh),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).

// (c) GENERIC THROUGH THE ARRAY SHORTHAND — the TypeScript form of the trap
// java/lambda.dl records at (i-b), where the variable hid under `Consumer<? super T>`
// and their depth-1 rule matched nothing. Here it hides under `T[]`: `xs: Alpha[]` is an
// ARRAY reference, never resolves to the Array declaration, and clause (b) finds no
// parameter to bind. ref_element_target already knows an array's element type.
// owner_declares_param keeps it honest — `filter`'s callback parameter is Array's own
// variable T and binds; `reduce`'s first is U, declared on the method, and does not.
lambda_param_type(lp, prov, t) :- callback_arg(ce, argpos, lm),
    ts_call_site(_, _, _, recv, _, ce, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _),
    recv != "",
    expr_decl_ref(recv, rref),
    expr_call_candidate(ce, callee),
    callee_fn_param_ref(callee, argpos, fref),
    fn_param_tvar(fref, k, pname),
    owner_declares_param(callee, pname),
    ref_element_target(rref, prov, t),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).

// ── and type the uses inside the body ───────────────────────────────────────
// The arrow's parameter has no annotation, so param_type is empty for it and this adds
// rather than competes. A parameter that IS annotated keeps its declared type.
expr_type(e, prov, t) :- expr_referenced("client", "PARAMETER", p, e),
    lambda_param_type(p, prov, t).

// ── …AND A CALLBACK THAT RECEIVES A MODULE NAMESPACE ────────────────────────
// `import("./m").then((m) => m.run(1))` — the same namespace as the awaited form, only
// reached through a callback. `await` is not required for a dynamic import to be
// followed, and the two spellings must resolve alike (#352).
//
// The parameter has no TYPE to give it: a module is not a type, which is why this is a
// namespace binding rather than a lambda_param_type clause. Keyed on `then` and on the
// receiver already being a namespace, so it cannot reach an ordinary promise.
lambda_param_namespace(lp, prov, m) :- call_site("client", _, "then", _, recv, ce, _),
    expr_namespace(recv, prov, m),
    callback_arg(ce, "0", lm),
    param_decl(_, _, "0", _, lm, lp).
expr_namespace(e, prov, m) :- expr_referenced("client", "PARAMETER", p, e),
    lambda_param_namespace(p, prov, m).

// ── an ARROW assigned to a VARIABLE annotated with a FUNCTION TYPE ──────────
// The rules above type a callback passed as an ARGUMENT, where the signature comes
// from the callee's parameter. This is the other way a lambda gets a contextual type,
// and nothing covered it:
//
//   export type DirectiveTransform = (dir: DirectiveNode, node: ElementNode,
//                                     context: TransformContext) => ...
//   export const transformBind: DirectiveTransform = (dir, _node, context) => { ... }
//
// None of `dir`, `_node` or `context` carries an annotation; all three are typed by
// the alias on the variable. Measured on one project's production code, 118 unresolved
// call sites have a receiver that is an unannotated parameter of an ARROW_FUNCTION,
// and this is the shape behind them — a typed handler, transform or visitor written
// as a const, which is the ordinary way to write one.
//
// `ref_shape_target` already walks the alias chain to the FUNCTION_TYPE reference, and
// `fn_param_type` already reads that reference's children positionally, so this adds a
// binding and no new traversal.
lambda_param_type(lp, prov, t) :- var_initializer("client", _, e, v),
    e != "",
    expr_anon_decl("client", lm, e),
    var_type_ref(_, r, v),
    ref_shape_target(r, fref),
    fn_param_type(fref, k, prov, t),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, lm, lp).

// ── an OBJECT-LITERAL METHOD takes its parameter types from the annotation ──
// `const ctx: Ctx = { push(code) { code.trim() } }` — `code` carries no annotation and
// is typed by `Ctx.push`'s first parameter. This is how an object literal implements an
// interface, which is the ordinary way to build a context, a visitor, a handler table
// or a plugin object.
//
// It needed a parser change to be expressible at all: an object-literal method used to
// be emitted with NO owner, so nothing connected it to the literal it belongs to. The
// owner is now the literal's EXPRESSION, which is the link this rule walks.
//
// Everything here sits below the expression fixpoint — the literal reaches its type
// through the variable's ANNOTATION, not through typing the literal as a value — so
// this can define param_type directly rather than going through lambda_param_type.
objlit_method_of_var(m, v) :- method_owner(_, e, m),
    method_kind(_, "OBJECT_LITERAL_METHOD", _, m),
    var_initializer("client", _, e, v).

objlit_contextual_param(mp, prov, t) :- objlit_method_of_var(m, v),
    method_decl(_, _, name, _, m),
    var_type_ref(_, r, v),
    ref_type_target(r, _, dt),
    scope_sibling(dt, sib),
    declared_method(sib, name, _, im),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, im, ip),
    param_type(ip, prov, t),
    param_decl(_, _, k, _, m, mp).

param_type(mp, prov, t) :- objlit_contextual_param(mp, prov, t).

// ── AN ASSERTION SIGNATURE TYPES ITS SUBJECT — `asserts x is T` ─────────────
// `assertDefined(x)`, `invariant(x)`, every hand-rolled schema check that throws. It is
// the only narrowing form whose purpose is to type code AFTER the call rather than
// inside a branch, so everything downstream of one is affected rather than one
// expression. Measured before writing this: the engine emitted NOTHING for the call
// after the assertion, because the subject keeps its declared type — usually `unknown`,
// which has no members to look up. #340.
//
// WHAT THIS IS NOT. The issue reports the type-GUARD form (`x is T`) as working and this
// as the sibling gap, implying narrowing machinery to extend. There is none: no rule
// reads a type predicate at all. The guard case resolves because its receiver is a
// UNION and the fan over both members happens to contain the compiler's answer — a
// sound superset, not a narrowing. So this adds the first predicate reading rather than
// extending one, and the guard form is left alone deliberately: narrowing it would
// REMOVE targets from a set that is currently sound, which is a different and riskier
// change than adding a type where there was none.
//
// FLOW-INSENSITIVE, AND SOUND BECAUSE OF WHAT IT ADDS TO. The asserted type is given to
// the subject throughout the enclosing function, not from the call onward — Datalog over
// positions has no statement order. That can only over-approximate, and only into a
// declared type that had no members: a use BEFORE the assertion does not typecheck, so
// the compiler names no target there for this to disagree with. The declared type is
// kept alongside, so nothing is removed.
//
// THE SUBJECT IS MATCHED BY NAME, NOT ASSUMED TO BE THE FIRST PARAMETER. `asserts x is
// T` names its subject, and the parameter whose name it is gets the type. Checked as a
// prefix — `"asserts " + name + " "` — so no index arithmetic is needed and a helper
// that narrows its second argument is handled by the same rule.
assertion_predicate(m, ctn) :- method_return_ref(prov, r, m),
    type_ref(prov, "TYPE_PREDICATE", _, _, ctn, _, _, r),
    strlen(ctn) > 8,
    substr(ctn, 0, 8) = "asserts ".
assertion_subject_pos(m, pos) :- assertion_predicate(m, ctn),
    param_decl(_, pname, pos, _, m, _),
    pname != "",
    pre = cat("asserts ", cat(pname, " ")),
    strlen(ctn) >= strlen(pre),
    substr(ctn, 0, strlen(pre)) = pre.
// The asserted type, off the predicate's own TYPE_PREDICATE_TARGET child.
assertion_asserted_type(m, prov, t) :- assertion_predicate(m, _),
    method_return_ref(rp, r, m),
    type_ref_parent(rp, r, _, child),
    type_ref(rp, _, "TYPE_PREDICATE_TARGET", _, _, _, _, child),
    ref_type_target(child, prov, t).
// The argument at that position, where it is a reference to a parameter of the caller.
assertion_narrowed_param(p, prov, t) :- expr_call_candidate(ce, m),
    assertion_subject_pos(m, pos),
    assertion_asserted_type(m, prov, t),
    expr_child("client", ce, "ARGUMENT", pos, ae),
    expr_referenced("client", "PARAMETER", p, ae).
param_type(p, prov, t) :- assertion_narrowed_param(p, prov, t).

// ── A BARE `asserts check` WHOSE SUBJECT IS A TYPE-PREDICATE CALL ──────────
// `assert(canCall(r), "no"); r.call("x")`. Two things the rules above do not cover: the
// assertion carries no `is T` of its own -- `asserts check` asserts nothing by itself --
// and its subject is a CALL rather than a reference to a parameter. What narrows `r` is
// the PREDICATE the subject calls, `canCall(r): r is Caller`, which the compiler applies
// to that predicate's own argument.
//
// This one was a WRONG answer rather than a missing one: the guarded call committed a
// single `known_edge` to the UNNARROWED declaration. The `asserts r is Caller` spelling
// of the same intent already worked, which is why it read as a one-off.

// A BARE `asserts <name>` — the prefix, and no ` is ` anywhere, so the two spellings
// cannot both fire for one method.
bare_assertion_subject_pos(m, pos) :- method_return_ref(prov, r, m),
    type_ref(prov, "TYPE_PREDICATE", _, _, ctn, _, _, r),
    strlen(ctn) > 8,
    substr(ctn, 0, 8) = "asserts ",
    !contains(" is ", ctn),
    param_decl(_, pname, pos, _, m, _),
    pname != "",
    ctn = cat("asserts ", pname).

// A PLAIN type predicate `x is T`, matched by subject NAME exactly as the asserts form
// is, so a predicate narrowing its second argument needs no index arithmetic.
predicate_subject_pos(m, pos) :- method_return_ref(prov, r, m),
    type_ref(prov, "TYPE_PREDICATE", _, _, ctn, _, _, r),
    !contains("asserts ", ctn),
    param_decl(_, pname, pos, _, m, _),
    pname != "",
    pre = cat(pname, " is "),
    strlen(ctn) >= strlen(pre),
    substr(ctn, 0, strlen(pre)) = pre.

predicate_target(m, prov, t) :- predicate_subject_pos(m, _),
    method_return_ref(rp, r, m),
    type_ref_parent(rp, r, _, child),
    type_ref(rp, _, "TYPE_PREDICATE_TARGET", _, _, _, _, child),
    ref_type_target(child, prov, t).

assertion_narrowed_param(p, prov, t) :- expr_call_candidate(ce, am),
    bare_assertion_subject_pos(am, apos),
    expr_child("client", ce, "ARGUMENT", apos, sub),
    expr_call_candidate(sub, pm),
    predicate_subject_pos(pm, ppos),
    predicate_target(pm, prov, t),
    expr_child("client", sub, "ARGUMENT", ppos, pae),
    expr_referenced("client", "PARAMETER", p, pae).
